#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128914 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5dab76a-9d0c-5625-bba5-f903a55e3b7d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Infinite Behavior of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "8ad1be49-273c-572d-8221-9605bfa16b7d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4012" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Which of the following is true when $x\\rightarrow 2?$   \n\nI. $f(x) \\rightarrow  \\infty$\n\nII. $f(x) \\rightarrow  2$\n\nIII. $f(x) \\rightarrow  -\\infty$\n\n![](images/33/33650d66a0f3a2fae762b1c19da952597740868b00eeec11a14ad822536962e8.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $2,$ the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty.$ \n\n![](images/45/4585f983dac68f3ac655fbaaed6481cd1479c4cffae2c10d9f08d991c6533f51.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128919 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129103 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "408f96cb-5b2d-532b-874b-f9441dddfa30" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing One-Sided Infinite Behavior of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "9d4d4b06-ace1-5d4a-828a-decea7c5b5fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4053" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d5750d5909f8a6e7c893109dd2ebe1e6317e7e5505df52d8be8966b8f914f140.png)\n\nThe graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) is shown above. Which of the following is true when ![](images/cf/cf6469e6430ca528386d02c3c62c8856abdd9cd592dab391ca0b37cb9eee214d.svg)\n\nI. ![](images/61/61e37acf991ed5b1c42f3c21005513060e0168597a24ca4fc0f3925a75fe77ad.svg)\n\nII. ![](images/6a/6ac2f1940e7398cde2efc3364d99ccbcba9cb95c761f338c1c643daa557cf804.svg)\n\nIII. ![](images/33/333a8ffca9eb9ad90f40e6a0a28a8119936d27bc840b0495c6b98eafec91d875.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The notation ![](images/e1/e15c0b105ea984c36aeb1eae6f4e743fd66d4a152acf6f2fdc5a9f977760e39f.svg) means that ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) approaches ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) *from the right*.\n\n![](images/99/99421620750831182ef45a4cfda34ee4b36f772f88df5572783bffc3ea9580c5.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) approaches ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) from the right, the values of ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) increase, approaching ![](images/b7/b7dcbb40b0c8f515a6b1ee8773cbf301ab1f40446114fd0e5436b57fd08e3697.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/33/333a8ffca9eb9ad90f40e6a0a28a8119936d27bc840b0495c6b98eafec91d875.svg) as ![](images/57/57038ceb8b89a8ded3292cf53d2875f5da509c824f4ad1cacd8ce1616f1cfff5.svg) So, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128920 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129104 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b81a25e-32d1-5f5f-9f8e-b18e5d354e2f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vertical Asymptotes"}] :question/id #uuid "5dee2a1e-7dd9-5c99-9f3b-5189360a2ef5" :question/math-academy-id "e-4054" :question/problem "Consider the graph of $y = f(x)$ shown below. What is the equation of its vertical asymptote?\n\n![](images/63/63fe6fd9a6a830b4f3f446d265ae57c24bec2e5f23917c4a5dda4000c63198e8.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $x = a$ is called a vertical asymptote of $y = f(x)$ if $f(x) \\to \\pm \\infty$ as $x \\to a^+$ or $x \\to a^-.$\n\n![](images/59/596435060b73c6df3b05bf5f56b4650c2e77ecb70203f3d680ad9d0dddc1f027.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty.$ Also, notice that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty.$\n\nSo, we can write: \n\n- $f(x) \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -1^-$\n\n- $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -1^+$\n\nThese give us the same asymptote $x = -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128921 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29ea673-5a84-5e48-b45d-85cade99566d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where a Function Approaches Infinity"}] :question/id #uuid "b6212c1b-c1c8-5d0d-9984-1509b721e54d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4062" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a28f89ca66be27ba85ded32668b7077cb2b152f9877fe52f6702a40be047773.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. Find the value of $b-a,$ given the following:\n\n- $g(x)\\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow a$\n\n- $g(x)\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow b$" :question/worked-solution "From the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1,$ the values of $g(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty.$ \n\n![](images/c5/c5b5723c08edacff5a29e160b225343f565d23bb764a84149ca20ebbe28d4ad3.png)\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x) \\to -\\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a = -1.$\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1,$ the values of $g(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty.$ \n\n![](images/3b/3bf9f0d83f05002952d531fe1dcaa6ad14d7d7c4216a59d9a2d3a4e621b57ef5.png)\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x) \\to \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b =1.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb - a = 1 - (-1) = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5dab76a-9d0c-5625-bba5-f903a55e3b7d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Infinite Behavior of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a30d4c1-4d5d-590f-ade6-b45bea586f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\to -\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547439} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2516f9-a12e-50a9-a436-094d782d8505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\to \\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547480} {:answer/id #uuid "8147ec1c-a795-511e-b8fe-56bae4f6295f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\to -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547593} {:answer/id #uuid "d10d022f-aa90-501d-848b-e62d1c08fe62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\to 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547765} {:answer/id #uuid "fb32aed5-ef18-5b85-a55f-3e72b4a11a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\to 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a2516f9-a12e-50a9-a436-094d782d8505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\to \\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547480} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf0e12d6-f84d-59c5-b35c-7d782639eeb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca47eb18-4aa6-5c60-add3-13a91c37460f" :question/math-academy-id "q-110669" :question/problem "![](images/ed/ed4ddeb8ea4f2bb9d05dc05d3ff73559806186d66a78431e89f50e3bbe390dd5.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is true when $x\\to 1?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/2a/2a84c7f64371af4728f86d83df8680ab85683cf4cf564ca9622d93b39fdfc267.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1$ the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129104 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b81a25e-32d1-5f5f-9f8e-b18e5d354e2f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vertical Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d50eb8e-496b-56e2-94e6-1a7eabb3b811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2,$ $x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547452} {:answer/id #uuid "cad7cccd-d43c-5829-961d-b9d7fe417a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547749} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2ec6c3-f1ec-5eb7-861b-cf4f0d59c3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547752} {:answer/id #uuid "e26b87f9-c50c-58e9-8f87-82fc0ac53da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547787} {:answer/id #uuid "f97ad6dd-e578-532a-99db-a0a68ce82607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cad7cccd-d43c-5829-961d-b9d7fe417a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547749} :answer-field/id #uuid "b475a21f-3809-5b3b-b4c4-760fa5caf3a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b6d62580-c7ef-590e-96ab-faddfa2655e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-111108" :question/problem "![](images/23/23015d804d6348d7ab6477fa981a1f2b24f43db4be96cca511ba53cfb53e49bd.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown above. What are the equations of its vertical asymptotes?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $x=a$ is called a vertical asymptote of $y=f(x)$ if $f(x)\\to \\infty $ or $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {a}^{+}$ or $x\\to {a}^{-}.$\n\n![](images/e6/e6d80874f9150371204f12b2e933085f9b2666ac77fce5430f9645e5af073a36.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$ Also, notice that as $x$ approaches $-2$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$\n\nWe also see that as $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$ And, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$\n\nSo, we can write: \n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -{2}^{-}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -{2}^{+}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{-}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}$\n\nThese give us the asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29ea673-5a84-5e48-b45d-85cade99566d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where a Function Approaches Infinity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2079e839-615d-50fe-94b0-a165953de47b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547381} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd51d86-d457-516e-ac62-cca692dc8851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-3$ and $a=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547493} {:answer/id #uuid "a09b73c8-cfe4-5dc1-9180-d67767a41575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-3$ and $a=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547666} {:answer/id #uuid "e6820c9f-6c5b-57d1-9cbd-0fb040cafa8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2$ and $a=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547794} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ef2269-b1c8-5c97-b7f3-12233daa93a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dd51d86-d457-516e-ac62-cca692dc8851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-3$ and $a=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547493} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e681c60-4b82-5c46-8f28-b7ad70bfc38f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d362e2bc-26cf-51c4-91b6-e3e5ba9e9850" :question/math-academy-id "q-39351" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d953b897affdad991b86db265b90610cb6e28f3ac6a94889c5143f2289d93f68.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Find the values ​​of $a$ such that $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to a.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/e9/e9862ea9f1debb162a62a3eb3422f2313f4a8512c30fe2a16e982207639c0ef4.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-3$ or $2$, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$ \n\nSo, we can write: $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -3$ and $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to 2.$ \n\nTherefore, $a=-3$ and $a=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129103 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "408f96cb-5b2d-532b-874b-f9441dddfa30" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing One-Sided Infinite Behavior of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b68ce78-1e8a-55c2-ba52-9b02c45bbf05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547487} {:answer/id #uuid "50463f52-843a-5163-8548-c96cd4408ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547499} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f0899a-c8da-5f22-b797-7b5f926af541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547672} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7cbe24-bd75-5065-be03-26434d9b0af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547715} {:answer/id #uuid "eae24aca-3888-5c4c-a5dd-905811119997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2f0899a-c8da-5f22-b797-7b5f926af541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547672} :answer-field/id #uuid "1019eed5-3538-5043-a33a-3c345b809f48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b21470c3-1ee9-52ab-ab28-1768b780f6d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-39451" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd6662d44cd356ee112fd0bec330d0cb85197935a312069ab766c46e0ef9c61d.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. As $x\\to -{2}^{-},\\,$ $f(x)\\to \\infty $\n\nII. As $x\\to {3}^{-},\\,$ $f(x)\\to \\infty $\n\nIII. As $x\\to {3}^{+},\\,$ $f(x)\\to -\\infty $" :question/worked-solution "The notation $x\\to -{2}^{-}$ means that $x$ approaches $-2$ *from the left*. In the same way, the notation $x\\to {3}^{-}$ means that $x$ approaches $3$ *from the left* while $x\\to {3}^{+}$ means that $x$ approaches $3$ *from the right*. \n\n![](images/37/37af0eeb28d271efa4bd8f296b8c530b82a7f368f82fa96e025695317b81519d.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that:\n\n- As $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ do not approach $\\infty .$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- As $x$ approaches $3$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$ So, statement II is true.\n\n- As $x$ approaches $3$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ do not approach $-\\infty .$ So, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129103 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "408f96cb-5b2d-532b-874b-f9441dddfa30" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing One-Sided Infinite Behavior of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01706240-bf16-5cd2-88a0-01b7e1068108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\to \\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547318} {:answer/id #uuid "30d1f4ce-cf97-5cfe-825a-1baaf9822d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\to 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547416} {:answer/id #uuid "62adc0cc-c742-59b0-bfaa-449ed5a524ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\to -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547531} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d641d7-2268-5da7-a256-4bf4823af319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\to -\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547767} {:answer/id #uuid "f614efbc-c627-550f-aa0b-b888808fd901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\to 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01706240-bf16-5cd2-88a0-01b7e1068108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\to \\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547318} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ab23431-35c4-5816-9f42-6807c5681847" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "936ec21a-6fd7-54a1-80fb-a5b7564ca5ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-39498" :question/problem "![](images/ed/ed3ed1aeb01e59fae6efa312fbbdfb476fa493df5a1dd5730e4e5c595827a8ea.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is true when $x\\to -{2}^{-}?$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $x\\to -{2}^{-}$ means that $x$ approaches $-2$ *from the left*.\n\n![](images/ef/ef61a0d8a90ec9d0b120b0b9bf47077c7c77887f457182c09ee36b7d78b8cf9f.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -{2}^{-}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129104 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b81a25e-32d1-5f5f-9f8e-b18e5d354e2f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vertical Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63cab16c-b0cd-5127-83bf-305e047b9217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1,$ $x=0$, and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547536} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef82baa-b2a6-5818-9426-d05b7a5450fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0,$ $x=1$, and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547635} {:answer/id #uuid "9836dd59-3429-506a-a5da-4a7f09061727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547648} {:answer/id #uuid "f51a01e1-db08-5916-a3de-61fd006cfd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547813} {:answer/id #uuid "f736d694-210e-5ab2-9a6d-00d1fc1ee568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f51a01e1-db08-5916-a3de-61fd006cfd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547813} :answer-field/id #uuid "4deadf1e-29b6-5a0e-9543-37299138f325" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1705d8ca-0ba8-5feb-91d5-1e7a97ff9132" :question/math-academy-id "q-39502" :question/problem "![](images/e3/e3dc597ee47ba7b86f51b37725a0998cb23b148a23448f8c6db21cbb60a0c350.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown above. What are the equations of its vertical asymptotes?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/73/73bbd191451c2b7769ccd2903fd0e6c9da81a11853071bbfbed9711267903b9e.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$ Also, notice that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$\n\nWe also see that as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$ And, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$\n\nSo, we can write: \n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -{1}^{-}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -{1}^{+}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {1}^{-}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {1}^{+}$\n\nThese give us the asymptotes $x=-1$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5dab76a-9d0c-5625-bba5-f903a55e3b7d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Infinite Behavior of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b12bab4-f22d-570a-963a-367b3ce27b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547442} {:answer/id #uuid "7691b709-f303-51b3-a0e5-4b140379f5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547575} {:answer/id #uuid "8c73f008-f517-5058-880f-1e0754701c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547627} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb07a79-05e8-5993-aa4e-fe8cc781da3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547664} {:answer/id #uuid "e30f8872-21e6-571a-87b6-f7748bb0eed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fb07a79-05e8-5993-aa4e-fe8cc781da3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547664} :answer-field/id #uuid "19fa27cb-d0cb-58be-ba1b-5007ddf4fbd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0274893d-bdb8-5547-900e-3f5b62ab65d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-66682" :question/problem "![](images/10/10ef921066e2984492588891cb36d52c142d387c94d23aa226e26377f30d96cd.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to 0$\n\nII. $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to 2$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to 2$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/68/68343d3b77ebad519983a98cc35531a632c85e453cb38a4583fb21cf4ea620e0.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that:\n\n- as $x$ approaches $0$ the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty $\n\n- as $x$ approaches $2$ the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29ea673-5a84-5e48-b45d-85cade99566d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where a Function Approaches Infinity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26f25a9e-e5df-512a-b13b-fe21656edc09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547388} {:answer/id #uuid "56f39e90-8e6b-5f4d-b80e-d0812afb28a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547509} {:answer/id #uuid "bede3045-9a6e-5cee-b982-d5ecaa7c3cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547722} {:answer/id #uuid "ccbe4206-79d2-56ec-9bc7-709ec0ce07c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547751} {:answer/id #uuid "dfedff98-e2a1-5c48-9f63-ccb6faf9d50f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56f39e90-8e6b-5f4d-b80e-d0812afb28a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547509} :answer-field/id #uuid "124c33fc-0540-5fef-b86c-da27bf036906" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12aa5ec1-b59f-559a-80d0-644a88af964f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73386" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bdce9d030776f147aa7a17881dc5369fc9e7f3a7f4776852d9f5d958ca526baa.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $h(x)$ is shown above. Find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}$ given that:\n\n- $h(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to a$\n\n- $h(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to b$" :question/worked-solution "From the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-2$ the values of $h(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$ \n\n![](images/02/02bb73c2c981a0fb9e128b89862b854f9fb6af6cca6ff216a69733561ae90dbf.png)\n\nSo, we can write: $h(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -2.$ \n\nTherefore, $a=-2.$\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $4$ the values of $h(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$ \n\n![](images/4c/4ce365f8ce995accf041aca6f1ac6ccc24d0b8e835e0d268c433ff244cfcfb10.png)\n\nSo, we can write: $h(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to 4.$ \n\nTherefore, $b=4.$\n\nFinally:\n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{4}{-2}=-2\n$$"}]}
